---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import PageBody from "../components/PageBody.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import OptionsTable from "../components/api/OptionsTable.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "introduction", label: "Introduction", heading: false },
  { id: "initialization", label: "Initialization" },
  { id: "options", label: "Options" },
  { id: "components", label: "Components" },
  { id: "ignoring", label: "Ignoring elements" },
  { id: "events", label: "Events" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="auto_init">
<PageBody sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.introduction}>
        <p class="flow-text">
          Auto Init starts all of the registered Expressive components with a single call.
          The IIFE bundle exposes it as <code>Expressive.AutoInit</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Importing the JavaScript installs a few document-level behaviors
          (Forms, Chips, Slider, Cards, ExpandingCard, Dialogs, BottomSheets,
          and SideSheets), but it does
          <strong>not</strong> call <code>AutoInit()</code> for you. Call it after the DOM is ready.
          This documentation site does that in <code>docs.js</code> on <code>DOMContentLoaded</code>.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.initialization}>
<Code code={`
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
  Expressive.AutoInit();
});
`} />
        <p>Or pass a root element and per-component options. The option keys are the component names from the table below.</p>
<Code code={`
Expressive.AutoInit(document.body, {
  Menu: {
    // pass options for Menu here
  },
  FloatingActionButton: {
    // pass options for FloatingActionButton here
  }
});
`} />
        <p>From the module build:</p>
<Code code={`
import { AutoInit, Tooltip } from './js/expressive.mjs';

AutoInit();

const element = document.querySelector('.custom-tooltip');
Tooltip.init(element, { position: 'top' });
`} />
        <p>Component <code>init()</code> methods also accept a <code>NodeList</code> or <code>HTMLCollection</code> and return an array of instances. An empty collection returns an empty array.</p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.options}>
        <p><code>AutoInit(context, options)</code> takes two arguments:</p>
        <OptionsTable>
            <tr>
              <td><code>context</code></td>
              <td>Element</td>
              <td><code>document.body</code></td>
              <td>DOM element to search within for components.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>options</code></td>
              <td>Object</td>
              <td><code>{"{}"}</code></td>
              <td>Options for each component, keyed by name. See that component's page for its own options.</td>
            </tr>
        </OptionsTable>
        <p>
          Calling <code>AutoInit()</code> again on the same elements is safe: each component's constructor
          destroys any existing instance on that element before creating a new one.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.components}>
        <p>
          These are the components <code>AutoInit()</code> starts, and the selector each one claims.
          Elements that also have <code>no-autoinit</code> are skipped.
        </p>
        <div class="docs-table-scroll">
        <table class="striped">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Name</th>
              <th>Selector</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>AppBar</code></td><td><code>header.medium, header.large, header:has(&gt; :is(nav:not(.tabs, .navigation-bar, .navigation-rail), .bar) &gt; .search-bar)</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Autocomplete</code></td><td><code>.autocomplete</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ButtonGroup</code></td><td><code>.button-group</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Cards</code></td><td><code>article:has(&gt; aside[id]:not([id=""])):not(:has(&gt; aside ~ aside)):has(&gt; button.card-reveal-trigger[type="button"], &gt; :not(aside) button.card-reveal-trigger[type="button"])</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ExpandingCard</code></td><td><code>article.expanding-card:has(&gt; dialog.expanding-card-dialog)</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Carousel</code></td><td><code>.carousel</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Chips</code></td><td><code>.chips</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Confetti</code></td><td><code>.confetti</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Particles</code></td><td><code>canvas.particles</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Keypad</code></td><td><code>.keypad, .keyboard</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ColorPicker</code></td><td><code>.color-picker</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>BarChart</code></td><td><code>.bar-chart</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>PieChart</code></td><td><code>.pie-chart</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>HeatmapChart</code></td><td><code>.heatmap-chart</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>RadarChart</code></td><td><code>.radar-chart</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>RadialChart</code></td><td><code>.radial-chart</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>MixedChart</code></td><td><code>.mixed-chart</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ColumnChart</code></td><td><code>.column-chart</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>CommandPalette</code></td><td><code>dialog.command-palette</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Questionnaire</code></td><td><code>form.questionnaire</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Datepicker</code></td><td><code>.date-picker</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>LineChart</code></td><td><code>.line-chart</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Menu</code></td><td><code>.menu-trigger</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>MessageScroller</code></td><td><code>.message-scroller</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Lightbox</code></td><td><code>.lightboxed</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>NavigationRail</code></td><td><code>.navigation-rail</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ScrollSpy</code></td><td><code>.scrollspy</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>FormSelect</code></td><td><code>select</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Tabs</code></td><td><code>.tabs</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Timepicker</code></td><td><code>.time-picker</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Tooltip</code></td><td><code>.tooltipped</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>FloatingActionButton</code></td><td><code>.fab-menu</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Menubar</code></td><td><code>.menubar</code></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Snackbar, CharacterCounter, and Slider stay out of this table. Slider still starts itself when the
          bundle loads. Forms, Chips, Cards, ExpandingCard, Dialogs, BottomSheets, and SideSheets also run an
          import-time <code>Init()</code>. Chips, Cards, and ExpandingCard appear in the table as well so a
          later <code>AutoInit()</code> can pick up elements added after load.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.ignoring}>
        <p>
          To make <code>AutoInit()</code> skip an element, add the class
          <code>no-autoinit</code>. Initialize that element yourself with the component's
          <code>init</code> method when you need options other than the defaults.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<a class="button tooltipped no-autoinit" data-tooltip="Hi">Hover</a>

Expressive.Tooltip.init(
  document.querySelector('.tooltipped.no-autoinit'),
  { position: 'top' }
);
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.events}>
        <p>
          Menu, Lightbox, NavigationRail, Cards and ExpandingCard fire DOM events from the
          element they were initialized on. <code>expressive:open</code> and
          <code>expressive:close</code> fire when the state changes, and
          <code>expressive:opened</code> and <code>expressive:closed</code> when the motion
          ends. Cards and ExpandingCard fire only <code>expressive:open</code> and
          <code>expressive:close</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each event comes right after the matching <code>on…</code> callback, which still
          works. The events bubble and cross shadow roots, so one listener on the document
          sees every component. Nested components bubble too: check <code>event.target</code>.
          Closing a Menu or Lightbox from its <code>expressive:open</code> listener stops it opening.
        </p>
<Code code={`
document.addEventListener('expressive:open', (event) => {
  if (event.target.matches('.menu-trigger')) analytics.track('menu-open');
});
`} />
        <p>Choices and changes carry what was chosen in <code>event.detail</code>:</p>
        <div class="docs-table-scroll">
        <table class="striped">
          <thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Fired by</th><th><code>detail</code></th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:change</code></td><td>Tabs, Carousel</td><td><code>{"{ panel }"}</code>, <code>{"{ item, dragged }"}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:select</code></td><td>Menu, Datepicker, Timepicker</td><td><code>{"{ item }"}</code>, <code>{"{ date }"}</code>, <code>{"{ hours, minutes }"}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:confirm</code>, <code>expressive:cancel</code></td><td>Datepicker, Timepicker</td><td>none</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:autocomplete</code></td><td>Autocomplete</td><td><code>{"{ values }"}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:chip-add</code>, <code>chip-select</code>, <code>chip-delete</code></td><td>Chips</td><td><code>{"{ chip }"}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:change</code></td><td>ButtonGroup, ScrollSpy (from the section), Tour</td><td><code>{"{ button, pressed }"}</code>, <code>{"{ link }"}</code>, <code>{"{ step, index }"}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:open</code>, <code>expressive:close</code></td><td>Tooltip, Snackbar, Tour</td><td>Tour's close: <code>{"{ completed }"}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:action</code>, <code>expressive:closed</code></td><td>Snackbar</td><td>none</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:copy</code></td><td>Copy buttons</td><td><code>{"{ text, copied }"}</code></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          An Autocomplete or Select input hosts a Menu, so it also fires that Menu's
          <code>expressive:open</code>, <code>expressive:close</code> and
          <code>expressive:select</code>. A callback that changes the choice again, such as
          an <code>onShow</code> that selects another tab, leaves only the event for the
          final choice.
        </p>
        <p>
          Tooltip fires <code>expressive:open</code> and <code>expressive:close</code> when it
          actually shows and hides, after its delays. A snackbar fires its events from the
          snackbar itself; <code>expressive:closed</code> comes just before it leaves the page.
          Tour's <code>expressive:close</code> says whether the Done button finished it.
        </p>
  </Section>
</PageBody>
</DocsLayout>
